# 05 Sealed fields
#
# Shows how a field is kept encrypted at rest.
#   - `sealed` stores the value in the tenant's encrypted payload. The
#     database holds ciphertext.
#   - `sealed indexed` adds a blind index: equality lookups run at SQL speed
#     and the key never enters the database.
#   - `unique` and `normalize(lower)` work on sealed fields too, through the
#     blind index. `normalize(lower)` makes "A@x.com" and "a@x.com" one value.
#   - `refinable` lets a caller narrow a read by equality on a sealed field.
#     Every read takes a refinement on its open fields with no flag; a sealed
#     field takes one only when marked `sealed indexed refinable`. `ordo
#     check` prints each read's refinable fields (docs/howto-cost.md).
#   - `std sealed` is one shape line. Without it the derived std ops
#     (`std.patients.get`, `.list`, `.update`) see the open fields only, so
#     granting them cannot disclose a sealed value. With it they see the
#     whole row. Hand-written ops are unaffected either way.
#   - Projecting a sealed field is work done by the host, row by row, after
#     the database hands the rows over. `take` bounds that work, and so does
#     the page a listing is served as. A sealed filter or sort key is not
#     bounded by either (see 14-flags-unguarded-unbounded.ordo).
#
# Reference: docs/definition-language.md section 3 "Sealed fields",
# section 2 "Text canonicalization", section 5 "Cost classes".

domain clinic @ 1.0.0

entity patients {
  ward:      text                                        # open: plain column
  admitted:  date                                        # open: plain column
  name:      text    sealed                              # encrypted
  email:     text?   sealed indexed unique normalize(lower)
  diagnosis: text    sealed
  mrn:       text    sealed indexed refinable
  std sealed                                             # derived std ops see the sealed fields
}

op registerPatient($ward: text, $admitted: date, $name: text, $email: text, $diagnosis: text, $mrn: text) =
  set patients { ward: $ward, admitted: $admitted, name: $name, email: $email, diagnosis: $diagnosis, mrn: $mrn }

# Equality on a `sealed indexed` field rides the blind index: no scan.
# This op projects open fields only. See the README: `ordo check` refuses
# an op that combines a blind-index lookup with a sealed projection.
op patientByEmail($email: text) =
  patients(email == $email)[ward, admitted]

# Projecting a sealed field opens it host-side for each row returned.
# `take 3` bounds that work, so no acknowledgment is needed.
op latestDiagnoses =
  patients[admitted, diagnosis]
  sort -admitted
  take 3

# Not shown: a filter on a sealed field that is NOT indexed, such as
# `patients(diagnosis == $d)`. The reference says it runs host-side and needs
# `unbounded`. Today `ordo check` reports `sealed_unsupported` for that op
# (see the README, "Findings"). Index the field to filter on it.
